     140Dead end
     141You are standing at the southern end of a long valley illuminated by
     141flickering red light from the volcanic gorge behind you. Carved
     141into the walls of the valley is an incredible series of stone faces.
     141Some of them look down into the valley with expressions of
     141benevolence that would credit a saint;  others glare with a malice
     141that makes the heart grow faint.  All of them are imbued with a
     141fantastic seeming of life by the shifting and flickering light of
     141the volcano.  The entire far end of the valley is taken up by an
     141immense carving of a seated figure;  its exact form cannot be seen
     141from here due to the uncertainty of the light.
     142You are standing at the north end of the Valley of the Stone Faces.
     142Above you, an incredible bas-relief statue of an immense minotaur
     142has been carved out of the rock.  At least sixty feet high, it sits
     142gazing down at you with a faint but definite expression of amusement.
     143You are standing at one end of a long corridor lined on each side
